  title: 'The Play That Nobody Wanted to Watch',
  grade: 'Class 8',
  emoji: '🎭',
  chapterText:
      'When the youth club decided to perform a street play about the dangers of child labour in the busy '
      'market square, Irfan expected the usual small, mildly curious crowd that gathered for such performances. '
      'Instead, most passers-by simply walked around the group of student actors without glancing twice, '
      'clearly assuming it was just another minor disturbance to avoid on their way to the vegetable stalls.\n\n'
      'Discouraged after their first two performances drew almost no audience, the group considered cancelling '
      'the remaining shows scheduled for that week. Their director, a college student named Reshma who '
      'volunteered with the club, suggested instead that they rethink their opening entirely, since the '
      'traditional slow introduction they had been using gave people no reason to stop walking in the first '
      'place.\n\n'
      'For their next performance, the group opened with a sudden, loud argument staged between two actors '
      'directly in the middle of the walking path, forcing curious pedestrians to pause and figure out what '
      'was happening before the play\'s actual message unfolded. This small change transformed everything: '
      'within minutes, a genuine crowd had gathered, drawn in by curiosity before they even realised they were '
      'watching a performance with a serious social message underneath.\n\n'
      'By the final performance that week, local shopkeepers were setting aside their own work briefly to '
